# EduBotics — arm-board permission floor on the Orange Pi 5 Pro.
# Covers BOTH arm families: the ROBOTIS OpenRB-150 boards (both OMX arms) and
# the edu6_studio Feetech arm's CH343P bridge. The filename keeps its historical
# "robotis" spelling on purpose — it is referenced by name from setup.sh and
# from pi_agent/constants.py::UDEV_RULE_NAMES (the agent's self-repair set).
#
# Unlike the Jetson (ONE arm → a fixed `edubotics-follower` symlink), the Pi has
# TWO identical OpenRB-150 boards: same VID 2F5D, same PIDs 0103 (default fw) /
# 2202 (alt fw). A static VID/PID rule CANNOT tell leader from follower, so this
# file deliberately does NOT create role symlinks — a shared symlink would point
# both slots at whichever board enumerated last (a silent leader/follower swap).
#
# The leader/follower ROLE mapping is resolved at scan time by pi_agent
# (servo-ID ping via identify_arm.py inside a throwaway scanner container: IDs
# 1-6 = leader, 11-16 = follower) and PERSISTED as each board's stable
# /dev/serial/by-id/... path in the managed .env keys FOLLOWER_PORT / LEADER_PORT
# (deploy plan §5). The by-id path is unique per physical arm and survives
# replug (the arms have DISTINCT stable serials — identical-serial is a
# camera-only problem, CLAUDE.md), so no dynamic serial-keyed symlink is needed.
#
# This rule's job is therefore only the PERMISSION FLOOR + a stable device tag:
# group=dialout + mode 0660 so the agent's scanner container and an interactive
# debug shell (a user in the dialout group) can open the ports. It is the
# last-resort access rule, NOT the role source. (FTDI is only a legacy udev
# fallback and is intentionally not matched here.)
#
# The edu6_studio arm family (edu6 §4.4) is the THIRD line below: ONE Feetech
# STS arm behind a Waveshare Bus Servo Adapter, whose bridge chip is a WCH
# CH343P (1a86:55d3, in-tree cdc_acm -> /dev/ttyACM*). Same doctrine: the line
# grants the permission floor only. The edu6 rig has no leader/follower pair to
# tell apart at all (its single arm always drives FOLLOWER_PORT), and its
# identity is PROVEN by the SERVOS answering a Feetech ping
# (`identify_arm.py --protocol=feetech`), never by the bridge chip — a CH343P is
# a generic USB-serial adapter and this rule must not be read as claiming
# otherwise. The VID/PID mirrors pi_agent/constants.py::ARM_USB_IDS["edu6"].
#
# Mitigating context for the whole file: docker-compose.opi.yml runs
# open_manipulator `privileged: true` with `/dev:/dev`, so the CONTAINER can
# open these nodes regardless. The floor matters for a non-root host-side
# debug shell and for any future non-privileged path.
#
# Reload after editing:
#   sudo udevadm control --reload-rules
#   sudo udevadm trigger
#
# NOTE: the running agent byte-compares the INSTALLED copy of this file against
# the one it ships and re-installs on drift (agent.py::_drifted_udev_rules), so
# an edit here reaches a fielded Pi via the normal self-update. A LOCAL
# workaround for a not-yet-shipped board must therefore go in a SEPARATE rules
# file — a hand-added line here counts as drift and is reverted.

SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="2f5d", ATTRS{idProduct}=="0103", GROUP="dialout", MODE="0660", TAG+="edubotics-arm"
SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="2f5d", ATTRS{idProduct}=="2202", GROUP="dialout", MODE="0660", TAG+="edubotics-arm"
SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="1a86", ATTRS{idProduct}=="55d3", GROUP="dialout", MODE="0660", TAG+="edubotics-arm"
